<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santa Marta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santa Marta is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of Colombia, founded by Rodrigo de Bastidas in 1525 and one of the oldest Spanish towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. It served as a base for the conquest of the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> died at the estate of San Pedro Alejandrino outside the city on 17 December 1830, as Gran Colombia was breaking apart.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santa-marta.jpg" alt="Santa Marta" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CristhianVarela · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bah%C3%ADa_de_Santa_Marta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>General Rafael Urdaneta seized power in Bogotá in September, hoping to recall Bolívar, but Bolívar died near <b>Santa Marta</b> on 17 December 1830.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada left <b>Santa Marta</b> on 5 April 1536 with about 800 Spaniards to follow the Magdalena River inland; disease, hunger and fighting killed most of them, and fewer than 180 reached Muisca territory in March 1537.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Bananas grown for the United Fruit Company near <b>Santa Marta</b>, oil from the fields near Barrancabermeja from 1921, and later coal, flowers and gold widened exports, and petroleum eventually became the leading export.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish contact began after the foundation of <b>Santa Marta</b> in 1525.</blockquote><a href="/state/tairona">Tairona</a></li></ul></section>
